# A2 joins "wretched" (S4–S5) with "more unfortunate" (S3). The text treats these as one notion, misery or unhappiness.
# One could read "wretched to will evil" as moral baseness and "unfortunate" as ill fortune. But Boethius' point is that wickedness is itself misery, so I read no shift and no bridge.
